---
/**
 * About page. Copy from copy-about.md (approved 9 November 2026); six changes
 * from the copy brief approved 10 November 2026.
 * Template from design-system.md, "About": Hero, SectionRibbon, SectionRows
 * (AI first), Languages row, then the ContactPanel and Footer from the layout.
 * Every row looks the same. The ribbon order MUST match the row order.
 */
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import headshot from '../assets/headshot.jpg';

/** Short ribbon names (one to three words), in row order. */
const ribbon = [
  { id: 'ai', label: 'AI' },
  { id: 'payments', label: 'Payments and platforms' },
  { id: 'founder', label: 'Founder' },
  { id: 'internal-teams', label: 'Internal teams' },
  { id: 'how-i-work', label: 'How I work' },
  { id: 'where-i-started', label: 'Where I started' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="Aadeel Peerally is a product leader based in Berlin, working on payments platforms, marketplaces, medical learning tools and solar software."
  ogImage="/og/about.png"
>
  <section class="ps-halo" aria-labelledby="about-heading">
    <div class="ps-container ps-hero__inner">
      <div class="ps-hero__text">
        <div class="ps-eyebrow ps-eyebrow--accent">About</div>
        <h1 class="ps-display-lg" id="about-heading">A product leader based in Berlin.</h1>
        <p class="ps-lead">
          My work has centred on products where software meets money, operations and data: payments platforms,
          marketplaces, learning tools for medical students and doctors, and software for solar developers. I have
          led product organisations of up to 12 people, and I stay hands-on in every role.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="ps-hero__media portrait-sm">
        <div class="ps-portrait">
          <Image
            src={headshot}
            alt="Portrait of Aadeel Peerally"
            width={420}
            widths={[210, 420]}
            sizes="210px"
            loading="eager"
          />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="ps-container">
    <nav class="ps-ribbon" aria-label="On this page">
      {ribbon.map((r) => <a href={`#${r.id}`}>{r.label}</a>)}
    </nav>

    <section class="ps-row" id="ai">
      <h2 class="ps-h2 ps-row__title">Working with AI</h2>
      <div class="ps-row__body">
        <p>
          At PVcase, I lead product for a platform that brings solar engineering and commercial workflows together.
          Retention stayed low, so I stopped work on notifications and business-development packages and interviewed
          customers. They were reading contracts, compliance documents and process documents outside the platform to
          build task lists and compliance checklists. That led to AI-Assisted Design Review. Customers upload their
          documents, an LLM proposes the checks, and a person approves them before they apply. It is released and
          gaining traction with customers, and retention more than doubled.
        </p>
        <p>
          I owned the decision to keep a human in the loop. User interviews made it clear that our customers did not
          yet trust the output of AI workflows on their own. Human validation lets them use the automation while
          staying in control of the result.
        </p>
        <p>
          I built Arnie OS, a system of 10+ AI agents for research, PRDs and reporting. Nothing is filed until I
          approve it. Senior PMs on my team use it for their own work. I built it to cut the repetitive research and
          reporting work my team and I were doing.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="ps-row" id="payments">
      <h2 class="ps-h2 ps-row__title">Payments and platforms</h2>
      <div class="ps-row__body">
        <p>
          From WeSwap, I moved to Equals, then to Delivery Hero and AMBOSS. Each company handled payments in its own
          way, shaped by how quickly it had grown.
        </p>
        <p>
          At Delivery Hero, I led the migration of 20+ countries onto one financial platform, working across
          regulatory, engineering and regional teams. I also worked with business teams on payment provider tenders
          and contract negotiations with Checkout.com, Adyen, Stripe, Mercado Pago and Asian providers. The work cut
          annual processing costs by €5M+. At AMBOSS, we brought a fragmented payment set-up together into one system.
          Processing costs fell by nearly 50%, payment success rates rose by 4%, and time-to-market for campaigns and
          new products fell from weeks to hours. In each case, the right answer depended on the business around it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="ps-row" id="founder">
      <h2 class="ps-h2 ps-row__title">Building something of my own</h2>
      <div class="ps-row__body">
        <p>Alongside WeSwap and Equals, I co-founded TryMyKitchen, a self-serve marketplace for cooking classes.</p>
        <p>
          Before TryMyKitchen, cooking class hosts listed their classes on general experience and deal sites, where
          they were easily lost among other offers. To stand out, hosts had to pay higher commissions or sell at steep
          discounts. We built a dedicated self-service portal for hosts, combined with paid and organic marketing
          designed to put their classes near the top of search results.
        </p>
        <p>
          The marketplace grew to 8,000+ classes across Europe and North America, with partners including Jamie Oliver
          cooking schools and Heineken. Hosts could now be found near the top of search results without paying higher
          commissions or selling at steep discounts.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="ps-row" id="internal-teams">
      <h2 class="ps-h2 ps-row__title">Internal teams are customers too</h2>
      <div class="ps-row__body">
        <p>
          Before I worked in product, I worked alongside the people who keep a product running. At WeSwap, I was Head
          of Operations. I led the customer support team, up to 11 agents, and the compliance and fraud teams.
        </p>
        <p>
          At WeSwap, I saw that internal teams are customers too. A product has to serve the people who use it and the
          people who support it. Support agents need tools that triage incoming queries, answer simple requests
          automatically and give customers self-service options. Without them, agents move from one call or email to
          the next, with no time to listen.
        </p>
        <p>
          The compliance and fraud teams faced a different version of the same problem. They handled a high volume of
          paperwork and failed applications caused by incorrect customer information. Some customers waited more than
          a week for their accounts to open. As Head of Operations, I drove integrations with Stripe, LexisNexis and
          Salesforce that improved transaction monitoring and cut platform fraud by over 90%. When I became Chief
          Product Officer, simplifying identity checks and
          making them as self-service as possible was an obvious priority. Accounts were approved much faster, and the
          team had more time for complex cases.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="ps-row" id="how-i-work">
      <h2 class="ps-h2 ps-row__title">How I work</h2>
      <div class="ps-row__body">
        <p>
          I stay hands-on in every role. I attend team ceremonies, take on initiatives and deliver to market alongside
          my team.
        </p>
        <p>I develop product managers through coaching and by giving them real work to learn from.</p>
        <p>
          My product principle is objective decision making. I use data, and I ask my team to weigh three inputs in
          every decision.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Customer insight.</strong> Listening to customers is central to building products. It does not mean
          building exactly what they ask for, so I weigh what they say against the other two inputs.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The reality of the business and its market.</strong> What are the business goals, and how do they
          cascade into each team's initiatives? Which market signals matter, and what could competitors do to win
          against us?
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Return on investment.</strong> Every piece of work is an investment of company funds in a bet, so I
          look at value against effort. AI now makes engineering faster and cheaper. Product teams have to discover,
          prototype, adapt and refine faster than before.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="ps-row" id="where-i-started">
      <h2 class="ps-h2 ps-row__title">Where I started</h2>
      <div class="ps-row__body">
        <p>
          I studied Telecommunications Engineering at King's College London, graduating with an MEng in 2005. My early
          roles were in sales and customer support, before I moved into product at the Guardian.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="ps-row" id="languages">
      <h2 class="ps-h2 ps-row__title">Languages</h2>
      <div class="ps-row__body">
        <p>English (native), French (fluent), Spanish (intermediate) and German (basic).</p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* Shorter portrait for the About headline (design-system.md, Hero rules). */
  .portrait-sm {
    width: clamp(160px, 17vw, 210px);
  }
</style>
